{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q007_chey_like_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 supports a shared superfamily-level lineage, not a shared family or function. All three units sit in fold c.23 (Flavodoxin-like, class c, alpha/beta) and superfamily c.23.1 (CheY-like). The CheY-related response-regulator receiver family is c.23.1.1 (sunid 52173). The ornithine decarboxylase (ODC) N-terminal 'wing' domain is family c.23.1.4 (sunid 52252, auto-mapped to Pfam PF03709). The N-terminal domain of KaiA is family c.23.1.5 (sunid 82344; the KaiA domain example d1m2ea_ carries CASP5 annotation). So the supported relationship is that these are sibling families within one CheY-like superfamily: they share the core flavodoxin-like alpha/beta architecture that SCOPe uses to group them. Because they fall in different families, SCOPe does not establish close family-level homology among them, and the grouping does not establish a common signaling mechanism or a common catalytic role.

Not supported by this lineage: (1) that the ODC N-terminal wing is a receiver or phosphorylation-based regulatory module, or that it contributes catalysis; the ODC catalytic machinery is classified separately (major domain c.67.1.5, C-terminal domain d.125.1.1), so catalytic claims should not transfer from the CheY-like membership. (2) That the KaiA N-terminal domain has receiver-type phosphorelay or aspartate-phosphorylation activity, or any specific role in the KaiABC clock beyond what a separate functional source would establish. (3) That CheY-like membership implies shared function, ligand/partner binding, or domain count and exact boundaries for any model. (4) That the response-regulator receiver fold equals a functional signaling module in the ODC or KaiA units.

Limits: the claim rests on SCOPe classification of reference domains, which is structural-evolutionary grouping; it is not a sequence-identity or function statement. The c.23.1.4 family annotation is automatic (Pfam mapping), and the KaiA example is a CASP5-era reference, so the family placement is weaker evidence than a curated assignment. Name-based searches for 'CheY-like', 'KaiA N-terminal domain', and 'ornithine decarboxylase N-terminal wing' returned no hits, so classification was resolved by SCCS/SUNID and token lookups. Any claim about a specific target sequence or AlphaFold model would still need its own structure or hit evidence.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop (token mode: ornithine decarboxylase; KaiA)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sccs c.23.1.1, parents)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sccs c.23.1.5, parents)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sunid 52252, parents and annotations)","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains (d1m2ea_, parents and annotations)","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.23.1.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.23.1.5/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52252/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52252/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1m2ea_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1m2ea_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ornithine+decarboxylase&mode=token&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=KaiA&mode=token&limit=10"]}]}
